WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:05.690
We are now in the section on complex computing
problems and will look at three categories

00:00:05.690 --> 00:00:13.180
of problems and solutions: math, datetime,
and random. We will start with math, and in

00:00:13.180 --> 00:00:22.110
this code file, 621-math, I have imported
the math module and declared two variables,

00:00:22.110 --> 00:00:27.420
one as a positive number with decimals and
the other as a negative number. Because there

00:00:27.420 --> 00:00:33.130
are so many math functions to cover in this
topic, we will take a break from gaming and

00:00:33.130 --> 00:00:39.970
focus solely on the purpose of teaching these
math functions. On line 6, I have the fabs

00:00:39.970 --> 00:00:48.230
method applied to y. Fabs means floating absolute,
and absolute is the absolute distance from

00:00:48.230 --> 00:00:57.340
zero, so when I run this, a positive number
with a decimal should be returned, and indeed,

00:00:57.340 --> 00:01:04.049
that is what we have. One positive thing
to keep in mind: all these functions are part

00:01:04.049 --> 00:01:11.220
of Python's standard library, so no additional
installations are required to use them. I

00:01:11.220 --> 00:01:17.970
will uncomment and comment code as needed
as I run these other math functions. The next

00:01:17.970 --> 00:01:24.979
two math functions are ceil and floor, on
lines 8 and 10. Ceil returns the next integer

00:01:24.979 --> 00:01:32.301
up after a decimal number, and floor returns
an integer without a decimal number. With

00:01:32.301 --> 00:01:38.479
that knowledge, what do you think the results
will be when I run these two lines of code?

00:01:38.479 --> 00:01:50.690
If you said 4 and 3, you are correct. 3.33
has a ceiling of 4 and a floor of 3. If I

00:01:50.690 --> 00:01:58.520
were to switch the x's to y's and rerun this,
the result would be -12 for both because no

00:01:58.520 --> 00:02:05.939
decimal number affects this integer. I will
leave these two statements uncommented for

00:02:05.939 --> 00:02:19.561
now and uncomment lines 12 and 14 for the
trunc function. I will set y to -12.5. The

00:02:19.561 --> 00:02:25.210
trunc function is similar to ceil and floor,
but let's run this and see if we can spot

00:02:25.210 --> 00:02:32.020
the differences among these functions. With
x, trunc returns the same result as the floor

00:02:32.020 --> 00:02:41.159
function. With y, trunc returns the same value
as for ceil, but floor is -13. I will change

00:02:41.159 --> 00:02:50.690
the ceil and the floor back to evaluating
x. With x, trunc returns the same value as

00:02:50.690 --> 00:02:56.690
floor. Here are the differences among these
functions: ceil and floor evaluate the number,

00:02:56.690 --> 00:03:03.330
not whether it is positive or negative. Trunc
returns the next highest positive number,

00:03:03.330 --> 00:03:12.379
like 4 for x, and the next lowest negative
number, like -13 for y.  In other words,

00:03:12.379 --> 00:03:16.700
trunc truncates towards zero, not necessarily
toward the next highest positive or lowest

00:03:16.700 --> 00:03:27.769
negative number. Speaking of 13, I will set
y to -13 and x to 3 for the next function,

00:03:27.769 --> 00:03:39.080
fmod, on line 16. Fmod returns the modulus,
what we know as a remainder, from dividing

00:03:39.080 --> 00:03:48.300
two numbers. What do you think the remainder
will be for dividing -13 by 3? I am thinking

00:03:48.300 --> 00:03:59.209
-1. Let's run this code, and the result is
-1 as a float, which is what the f represents

00:03:59.209 --> 00:04:08.900
in the function. For those of you into statistical
functions, the frexp function, on line 18,

00:04:08.900 --> 00:04:15.180
returns the mantissa, the fractional part
of a logarithm, and the exponent of a number.

00:04:15.180 --> 00:04:19.910
I will run the frexp function for x and see
its mantissa and exponent.

